<PageLayout title="A useful website brief starts with a purpose."><main id="main" class="resource-main"><a class="text-link" href="/resources/">← Back to resources</a><p class="eyebrow">PLANNING / <Wordmark inline/> JOURNAL</p><h1>A useful website brief starts with a purpose.</h1><p>You don’t need to arrive with every page written. Start with what your business does and what you want a visitor to do next.</p><h2>Choose the main action</h2><p>Is the website meant to generate inquiries, explain a service, show your work, or sell a product? A clear priority helps shape the navigation and the content.</p><h2>Bring the essentials</h2><ul><li>A short description of your business and the people you serve.</li><li>The services, products, or projects you want to feature.</li><li>Your logo, photographs, colors, and any copy you have permission to use.</li><li>The contact details and business information you want to publish.</li><li>Your preferred timeline and a budget range to discuss.</li></ul><h2>Show what appeals to you</h2><p>References help most when you explain the detail you like: the lettering, the space around the content, the navigation, or the atmosphere. They are a starting point for an original design.</p><a class="button" href="/request/">Put your brief together →</a></main></PageLayout>